  <h2>What is balance?</h2>

  <p>
    Balance is the ability to remain athletic while you move.
  </p>

  <p>
    Every swing begins with movement. The challenge isn't staying perfectly
    still. The challenge is staying under control while your body prepares to
    move forward.
  </p>

  <p>
    There is no single weight distribution that is perfect for every hitter.
  </p>

  <p>
    Some hitters feel better with slightly more weight on the back leg. Others
    prefer to stay closer to even. For most hitters, somewhere around
    <strong>50–60% on the back leg</strong> creates an athletic position that
    is ready to move in either direction.
  </p>

  <p class="emphasis">
    The goal isn't a number.
    <br />
    The goal is to remain explosive.
  </p>

  <p>
    A balanced hitter stays centered.
  </p>

  <p>
    The head remains in a neutral position, allowing both eyes to track the
    baseball as long as possible.
  </p>

  <p>
    Pressure can move into the back side without allowing the entire body to
    drift there.
  </p>

  <p>
    Likewise, the front side should remain strong instead of collapsing before
    the swing begins.
  </p>

  <p>
    Balance is not about freezing.
  </p>

  <p>
    It's about staying athletic enough to do whatever the next moment requires.
  </p>

  <p>
    A good load should strengthen your balance.
  </p>

  <p>
    Good balance should strengthen your firing sequence.
  </p>

  <p>
    Every pillar prepares the next.
  </p>

  <p class="final-thought">
    Don't overcommit in either direction.
    <br />
    Remain athletic.
    <br />
    Remain explosive.
  </p>
</ChapterLayout>